WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.120
Pack seven red counters and four blue

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.350
counters into trays.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.800
A tray holds at most four counters, and at most two of

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:08.630
them may be red.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.210
What is the smallest number of trays needed?

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.260
There are eleven counters.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.710
Each tray holds at most four, so two trays

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.400
are not enough.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.380
At least three trays are needed.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.780
But that is only a first limit.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.580
Now look at the red counters alone.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.140
Each tray takes at most two reds.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.660
Three trays could hold only six reds,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:33.560
and there are seven.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.680
So at least four trays are needed.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.220
This limit is stronger.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.660
Can four trays work?

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:43.830
Fill them like this: two reds and two blues; two reds

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.300
and one blue; two reds and one blue; and one red.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.090
All eleven counters are packed and every rule is obeyed.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.520
So four trays are enough, and three are not.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.020
The answer is four.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.620
Notice that some spaces are left empty.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.900
That is fine: the red rule, not the total space,

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.160
decided the answer.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:06.040
Check each rule for a lower bound.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.170
Take the strongest bound, then build a packing

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.100
that reaches it.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.480
Try it with nine red counters and three blue counters.
